What is an Interface?

Interface is similar to class which does not have any implementations.
It is a way to achieve 100% abstraction in java.
It is a way to achieve multiple inheritance.
it is a way to achieve loose coupling in java ----> Loose cpouplng is a design pattern which promotes
 a system where each of its components has, or makes use of, little or no knowledge of the definitions of
 other separate components.

 Each and every method in interface is public by default.
 Each and every variable in Interface is public, static or final by default.

 From Java8 onwards we can have default and static methods in the interface, but they do not be a
 part of the abstract methods

In Layman Interface serves as a contact between the class and outside world.
If a class implements the interface, it is bound to provide the implementation of all the methods declared in
the interface

Different types of interfaces
1.FUnctional Interface----> we can have only one abstract method. 80% of JAVA works on abstract methods
2.Normal Interface
3.Marker Interface---> replaced it with annotations
